7.What is the difference between // and / in XPath?

•Scope of Search:

/ (Single Slash): Searches from the root node or direct children of the current context.
   It is used for specifying an exact location in the document.
// (Double Slash): Searches across all descendants of the current node, regardless of depth. 
   It is used for a more flexible and broad search.

Starting Point:

/: Always starts from the root of the document when placed at the beginning of the expression.
//: Can start searching from any point in the document, making it more versatile for locating 
    elements deep in the hierarchy.

Performance Considerations:

/: Generally more efficient when you know the exact location of the element because it does not
   traverse the entire document.
//: Can be less efficient, especially in large documents, because it searches through all nodes 
    to find matches, potentially traversing a large portion of the document.

